---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const modules = import.meta.glob('../content/factors/*.md', { eager: true });
const factors = Object.values(modules)
  .map((m: any) => m.frontmatter)
  .sort((a: any, b: any) => a.number - b.number);

const foundation = factors.filter((factor: any) => factor.original);
const additions = factors.filter((factor: any) => !factor.original);
---

<Base title="The Twenty-Two-Factor App — A field guide for modern software">
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="hero-copy">
      <p class="eyebrow"><span>Field guide 01</span> Software that survives contact with reality</p>
      <h1>12 has become 22.</h1>
      <p class="hero-lede">
        Ten durable ideas from the original twelve. Twelve missing principles for
        security, reliability, delivery, data, correctness, and sustainability. One
        opinionated field guide for software people can actually operate.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="button button-primary" href="/factors/codebase">Start with Factor I <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
        <a class="button button-secondary" href="#why-22">See what changed</a>
        <a class="button button-secondary" href="/audit">Audit a project</a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="hero-plate" aria-label="Twelve original factors minus two retired factors plus twelve new factors equals twenty-two">
      <div class="plate-topline"><span>Revision</span><strong>2026.3</strong></div>
      <div class="plate-number" aria-hidden="true">22</div>
      <div class="plate-equation">
        <span><b>12</b> original</span>
        <i>−</i>
        <span><b>2</b> retired</span>
        <i>+</i>
        <span><b>12</b> missing</span>
      </div>
      <div class="plate-status"><i aria-hidden="true"></i> Comparative edition · open source</div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="manifesto" aria-label="Methodology summary">
    <p>
      This is not a vendor checklist. It is a set of architectural pressures:
      make the right thing easy, make unsafe states hard to reach, and leave
      enough evidence for the next person to understand what happened.
    </p>
    <span class="manifesto-mark" aria-hidden="true">↘</span>
  </section>

  <section class="why-section" id="why-22">
    <div class="section-intro">
      <p class="section-label">01 · The argument</p>
      <h2>A respectful edit,<br />not a ceremonial sequel.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="why-grid">
      <article>
        <span class="mini-index">Keep / 10</span>
        <h3>Philosophy: Preserve what still compounds</h3>
        <p>
          Codebase discipline, explicit dependencies, external config, attached
          services, immutable releases, stateless execution, horizontal scale,
          disposability, parity, and one-off administration remain excellent
          constraints. We kept them—and made every page more testable.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="mini-index">Retire / 02</span>
        <h3>Philosophy: Promote intent over mechanism</h3>
        <p>
          Port binding was a useful 2011 deployment contract, but not every modern
          workload listens on a port. Logs-as-streams was a useful platform rule,
          but logs are only one observability signal. Their intent now lives in
          contract-first interfaces and observability with SLOs.
        </p>
        <a class="text-link" href="/retired">Read the retirement notes <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="mini-index">Add / 12</span>
        <h3>Philosophy: Cover the production blind spots</h3>
        <p>
          Hoffman’s Fifteen-Factor App identified interface-first design, telemetry,
          and identity. Reactive, SRE, Chaos, DORA, secure-by-design, GitOps,
          privacy, Local-first, FinOps, and sustainable-software work expose the
          remaining gaps. The new twelve compress those lessons into testable rules.
        </p>
        <a class="text-link" href="/research">Follow the research trail <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="factors-section" id="factors">
    <div class="section-intro factors-intro">
      <div>
        <p class="section-label">02 · The field guide</p>
        <h2>Twenty-two pressures.<br />No maturity theater.</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        Each factor starts with one imperative commandment and one boundary that
        prevents a common over-reading. The article then explains the constraint,
        names failure modes, and ends with a litmus test. Adopt the pressures as a
        system; adapt their implementation to context.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="factor-group">
      <div class="factor-group-heading">
        <div>
          <span class="group-number">01—10</span>
          <h3>The durable ten</h3>
        </div>
        <p>Original principles that still earn their place.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="factor-grid">
        {foundation.map((factor: any) => (
          <a class="factor-card foundation-card" href={`/factors/${factor.slug}`}>
            <div class="card-topline">
              <span class="card-number">{factor.numeral}</span>
              <span class="card-category">{factor.category}</span>
            </div>
            <h4>{factor.title}</h4>
            <span class="card-commandment-label">Commandment</span>
            <p>{factor.commandment}</p>
            <span class="card-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="factor-group additions-group">
      <div class="factor-group-heading">
        <div>
          <span class="group-number">11—22</span>
          <h3>The missing twelve</h3>
        </div>
        <p>Modern obligations the original methodology did not name.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="factor-grid">
        {additions.map((factor: any) => (
          <a class="factor-card addition-card" href={`/factors/${factor.slug}`}>
            <div class="card-topline">
              <span class="card-number">{factor.numeral}</span>
              <span class="card-category">{factor.category}</span>
            </div>
            <h4>{factor.title}</h4>
            <span class="card-commandment-label">Commandment</span>
            <p>{factor.commandment}</p>
            <span class="card-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="audit-promo" aria-labelledby="audit-promo-title">
    <div>
      <p class="section-label">03 · Put it to work</p>
      <h2 id="audit-promo-title">Find evidence.<br />Keep the judgment.</h2>
      <p>
        The Rust auditor inspects a repository or GitHub organization for
        factor-scoped evidence, then creates a versioned assessment for the
        decisions automation cannot make. It never compresses architectural risk
        into a flattering score.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="button button-primary" href="/audit">Open the audit guide <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
        <a class="button button-secondary" href="https://github.com/22-factor-apps/22-factor-apps-audit" rel="noopener noreferrer">View the Rust CLI ↗</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <ol class="audit-promo-steps">
      <li><span>01</span><strong>Discover</strong><p>Locate reviewable evidence without claiming that a filename proves compliance.</p></li>
      <li><span>02</span><strong>Assess</strong><p>Record status, rationale, evidence, owner, review date, and follow-up per factor.</p></li>
      <li><span>03</span><strong>Revisit</strong><p>Pin the edition and source release so the decision remains reproducible.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="evidence-section">
    <div class="section-intro evidence-intro">
      <div>
        <p class="section-label">04 · Research trail</p>
        <h2>Compared, challenged,<br />then compressed.</h2>
      </div>
      <a class="button button-dark" href="/research">Open the comparison</a>
    </div>

    <div class="evidence-grid">
      <a href="https://www.oreilly.com/library/view/beyond-the-twelve-factor/9781492042631/" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        <span>Twelve → Fifteen</span>
        <h3>Interfaces. Telemetry. Security.</h3>
        <p>The direct extension—and the starting point, not the end, of this revision.</p>
      </a>
      <a href="https://www.reactivemanifesto.org/" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        <span>Reactive · SRE · Chaos</span>
        <h3>Responsiveness must survive failure.</h3>
        <p>Reliability is a user-visible claim to test under realistic disturbance.</p>
      </a>
      <a href="https://dora.dev/capabilities/" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        <span>Formal methods · Functional design · Safe languages</span>
        <h3>Make invalid states difficult to express.</h3>
        <p>Model critical transitions, keep effects explicit, and let types force every case into view.</p>
      </a>
      <a href="https://cloud.google.com/transform/from-the-twelve-to-sixteen-factor-app" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        <span>Local-first · Sustainable · AI</span>
        <h3>Agency and quality survive the cloud.</h3>
        <p>Specialized manifestos pressure-test the universal rules without bloating them.</p>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="closing-cta">
    <p class="section-label">Use it, challenge it, improve it</p>
    <h2>A methodology should be<br />a conversation, not scripture.</h2>
    <p>
      The original twelve became useful because teams argued with them in public.
      This project is MIT-licensed and intentionally opinionated. Bring evidence.
    </p>
    <div class="hero-actions">
      <a class="button button-primary" href="/factors/codebase">Read Factor I <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      <a class="button button-secondary" href="https://github.com/22-factor-apps/22-factor-apps.github.io/issues" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open a discussion ↗</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
